impl=("Nothing in the SED, spectroscopic, astrometric or RV data contradicts the bound hierarchical triple. Each test is either neutral or shows no detectable difference between the two hypotheses at the relevant separation. "
"The case for the triple is therefore a prior-odds argument. Given an EB host within ~1.2 arcsec and a red (K5-M2) eclipsed star, a bound companion is ~10^2-10^3 times more likely a priori than a chance-aligned EB (conservative floor ~25-36). "
"It is strengthened by one weak extra factor of ~5-7: only 15-20% of red background hosts would sit at the 265-345 pc main-sequence distance implied by the eclipse-shape modelling. "
"The astro data constrain the companion only loosely. Its TESS light fraction is 1.2% (depth <= 50%) to <~6-12% (SED 95% limits), and >=2.5% if Gaia's C* excess is attributed to it. Most bound orbits at a <~10 au are excluded by PMA/RV/RUWE, so the companion is wider than ~0.03-0.1 arcsec, consistent with the 0.5-1.2 arcsec (150-360 au projected) Gaia-IPD window. "
"Data that would settle it: (i) AO or speckle imaging resolving a companion of dI~3.7-4.7 / dKs~2-2.6 at 0.5-1.2 arcsec, plus a second epoch for common proper motion (relative motion of a background star ~38 mas/yr); "
"(ii) red-optical or NIR spectroscopy detecting the M-dwarf EB lines (K ~ 100 km/s at 1.3 d) and a systemic velocity matching the G star's -6 km/s; (iii) Gaia DR4 epoch astrometry and photometry.")
cav=("1. SED: synthetic BT-Settl photometry with fixed floors. The 21-band chi2 mixes heterogeneous catalogues and is not null-calibrated; only the 9-band (Gaia+2MASS+WISE) test has a twin null. Twins were pre-selected to be clean (ipd_frac_multi_peak<=5, RUWE<1.3), which makes the C* and blending comparisons somewhat optimistic. GALEX NUV (-0.36 mag) and SkyMapper u (-0.16) residuals were excluded from the fit; they may reflect chromospheric or model UV issues. "
"2. The C*-to-companion-flux conversion assumes the excess is due only to a companion inside the BP/RP window, and that a fraction g (unknown) of its light is excluded from G. "
"3. GSP-Spec flag interpretation follows the DR3 datamodel (fluxNoise=1: 100<sigma_Teff<=250 K class). MatisseGauguin and ANN disagree by ~390 K, and neither is calibrated for composite spectra. "
"4. PMA limits depend on assumed per-transit RV error (2.65 km/s), per-epoch AL error (0.12 mas), a RUWE>1.4 cut (conservative given the observed 1.026), companion G-light 3%, and formal TGAS errors (field-calibrated z-scale 0.75-0.85, so conservative). The Tycho-2 mean-epoch test has a ~2 sigma RA hint (p=0.10) that is not significant. "
"5. Priors: Raghavan log-normal for a G primary and flat q are applied to a companion that is itself a 1.3-d binary. Kepler/TESS EB rates and depth distributions are for diluted catalogue EBs. The tertiary enhancement E is set to 1 or 3 by hand. Background counts are from Gaia at 1-30 arcmin and ignore stars Gaia misses near bright stars. The bound window lower edge (0.1 arcsec) is a crude stand-in for the PMA limits. The odds do not include the eclipse-shape, colour, or distance likelihoods. "
"6. TRICERATOPS: (a) both S and B priors use a fixed 2.2 arcsec limit when no contrast curve is supplied; the background prior uses a square area of (2.2 arcsec)^2 and counts all field stars to G=21; the bound prior is the companion fraction out to 654 au, with no requirement that the companion be a close binary. (b) It fits a single folded eclipse: at P=0.6518 d the folded profile averages the 5900 and 2700 ppm eclipses into one 4300 ppm twin-EB model, and at P=1.3037 d the secondary eclipse is masked. (c) It ignores the Gaia RV, the chromatic depth ratios, the SED, C*/IPD and parallax-distance consistency. (d) Evidences are heavy-tailed Monte Carlo estimates: 2e5-draw runs disagree from seed to seed; only H1 (1.32e7 draws) is converged to ~+-0.02 in probability. H2 SEB is carried by 1-2 chunks and its best solution is unphysical. (e) The background population is a Gaia-based file (TRILEGAL was unreachable through the proxy), 562 stars per 0.1 deg2. "
"7. In the task wording, HEBx2P corresponds to TRICERATOPS's SEBx2P (unresolved bound companion is the EB, at twice the folded period).")
